### The browser SDK: `@angular/fire` follows Angular, `firebase` follows `@angular/fire`

`@angular/fire` supports exactly one Angular major, and carries `firebase` as its own dependency — so the root `package.json` declares the `@angular/fire` release built for this workspace's **installed** Angular major, and `firebase` as **exactly the range that release lists** (`npm view @angular/fire@<version> dependencies.firebase`). Any other `firebase` makes the package manager nest a second SDK under `@angular/fire` — two Firebase SDKs, and `No Firebase App '[DEFAULT]'` at runtime. **Moving Angular a major means moving both together**, by those two rules. A **new** Angular + Firebase workspace is created at the newest Angular major that has a stable `@angular/fire` (the scaffold says which, and why). In an existing workspace, if no stable `@angular/fire` exists for its Angular major yet, the house refuses to pick one and says which choices you have (a deliberate prerelease, or the newest Angular that has one); whatever you declare, the generators keep — and every upgrade names a `firebase` that disagrees with the installed `@angular/fire`.

### Where each Firebase service is provided — and what it costs

**A Firebase service is provided where it is USED, not once at the root by default.** Each has its own generator-owned file under `{{APPS_DIR}}/<app>/src/app/`:

| Provider | File | Measured initial bundle with THIS service and nothing else |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `provideAppFirebase()` | `firebase.config.ts` | **238 kB** — the Firebase app itself (207 kB with no Firebase at all). **Always at root**: each service below resolves the default Firebase app from the SDK when its factory runs, so it must already be initialised at or above wherever the service is provided. |
| `provideAppAuth()` | `firebase-auth.config.ts` | 342 kB (app-check rides in with it) |
| `provideAppFirestore()` | `firebase-firestore.config.ts` | 413 kB — the heaviest |
| `provideAppStorage()` | `firebase-storage.config.ts` | 336 kB |
| `provideAppFunctions()` | `firebase-functions.config.ts` | 327 kB |
| *all four at root* | | **479 kB** — what `provideAppFirebase()` used to return on its own |

**Those per-service numbers do not add up, and that is the point.** Whichever service arrives first drags in Firebase's shared core, so the first one costs ~89–174 kB and each one after it is far cheaper. The saving is largest for a bundle that needs **no** service on the critical path, and shrinks — without vanishing — once one is there. (Raw, uncompressed, measured on a freshly scaffolded house app with no features.)

Provide each one either in **`app.config.ts`** (root — it is then in the initial bundle, which is correct for something bootstrap genuinely needs) or in the **`providers` of a lazily-loaded routes file** (it then rides that lazy chunk and never touches the critical path). A newly scaffolded app wires only `provideAppFirebase()` and leaves the four as a commented menu in `app.config.ts` — so **the first `inject(Firestore)` in a fresh app throws `NullInjectorError` until you place it.** That is deliberate: the alternative is every app paying ~240 kB for services it may never call.

**If this project predates nx-tools 0.33.0, your `app.config.ts` already has all four at root.** Migration `0.33.0/split-firebase-service-providers` put them there deliberately, so the split changed nothing about your bundle — it only made the choice expressible. Claiming the saving is your move, at your pace: delete the services this app never injects, and move the rest down into the lazily-loaded routes file that uses them.

**Two traps worth knowing:**

- **A providers array in the EAGER `app.routes.ts` does not defer anything.** A static import is what pins a chunk, so `providers: [provideAppFirestore()]` written in the eagerly-loaded routes file lands in the initial bundle exactly as if it were at root. It must be the routes file behind `loadChildren`.
- **A guard named in the eager `app.routes.ts` pins Auth into the initial bundle** — it is statically imported there, so its `inject(Auth)` drags `@angular/fire/auth` in wherever the provider itself is declared. An app that gates its routes on sign-in has Auth on its critical path by construction: provide it at root and accept the bytes honestly, or move the guard into the lazy routes file too. (Route providers *are* visible to that route's own `canActivate` — only a **child** route's providers are invisible to a parent's guard, so don't reach for root on a DI argument.)

### Per-service emulator toggling

You don't have to emulate everything or nothing — each Firebase service is independently emulated-or-real, so you can run e.g. **Firestore on the emulator but real Auth**.

- **Committed default (whole team):** the `EMULATE` map at the top of `{{APPS_DIR}}/<app>/src/environments/environment.ts`. Flip any service to `false`, save, the dev server hot-reloads (~1s).
- **Per session (just you, no rebuild):** override via URL query or localStorage —
  - `?emulate=firestore,storage` → ONLY these emulated this tab (others real); `all` / `none` work too
  - `?real=auth` → force Auth to the real backend this tab
  - `localStorage.setItem('emulate','firestore')` → persist per browser (`removeItem('emulate')` to drop it)
  - precedence (later wins): committed default **<** localStorage **<** URL query
  - these are the **browser's**: server-side rendering follows the stack's own switches (`DEV_URL_QUERY`, e.g. `{{#nx-serve}}--no-emulators{{/nx-serve}}{{^nx-serve}}--skip=emulators{{/nx-serve}}`), not a tab's

**Turning a service real needs real credentials.** A service resolved to real uses the `firebase` block of the active environment file — the `demo-<workspaceName>` values only work against the emulator. Fill `firebase` with your real/**staging** web config (`firebase apps:sdkconfig WEB <appId> --project <staging>`; never production for anything you write to). **If you forget, a dev-only `ngDevMode` guard (stripped from prod) logs a loud `firebase.config.ts` console error at bootstrap** naming the real-but-demo-configured services — so the cause is up front instead of only a cryptic `auth/api-key-not-valid` later. It logs rather than throws: the app still loads (one misconfigured service shouldn't brick the whole dev app), but that service won't authenticate against the real backend until you fill in real config. One coupled caveat, now handled for you: a real `projectId` is then also used by any *still-emulated* services (`singleProjectMode`), so the emulator suite must run under it too — `tools/emulators.sh` does this automatically by deriving its `--project` from `environment.ts` (override with `FIREBASE_EMULATOR_PROJECT`). The resolver is `{{APPS_DIR}}/<app>/src/app/emulator-overrides.ts` (generator-owned); `firebase.config.ts` resolves `committed-default ⊕ override` per service and each `firebase-<service>.config.ts` applies it, gated on `ngDevMode` — Angular's dev-mode flag, which the optimizer folds to `false` in production builds, so the resolver, the per-service wiring, every `connect*Emulator(...)` call, and all emulator addresses are tree-shaken out of the prod artifact. (Firebase's own SDK still carries its internal emulator support — that's library code, not ours.)

`{{SERVE}} <app> {{#nx-serve}}--no-emulators{{/nx-serve}}{{^nx-serve}}--skip=emulators{{/nx-serve}}` is the extreme of the same knob: **no** emulator suite booted and **every** service real. It carries `?emulate=none` to the app (the `emulator-overrides.ts` resolver maps `none` / `real=all` → all services real) and, like the per-service toggles above, resolves each service against `environment.ts`'s `firebase` block — the same "go real" path, no separate `no-emulators` environment file or build configuration.
{{/angular}}

### Cloud Functions ({{FUNCTIONS_ROOT}})

Cloud Functions are a first-class Nx app: `{{NX}} build {{FUNCTIONS_PROJECT}}` (esbuild) bundles `{{FUNCTIONS_ROOT}}/src/main.ts` into `dist/{{FUNCTIONS_ROOT}}` with a generated deploy-manifest `package.json`; the emulator and `firebase deploy` both consume that dist output (`firebase.json` → `functions.source`). Runtime deps (`firebase-admin`/`firebase-functions`) live at the **workspace root** — no per-project `node_modules`. The deployed runtime is `engines.node` in `{{FUNCTIONS_ROOT}}/package.json`, seeded from the project's Node (the nearest Cloud Functions runtime when Functions has none for it); every upgrade names a disagreement between the two and never rewrites it — moving a deployed runtime is your call. Deploy with `{{NX}} run {{FUNCTIONS_PROJECT}}:deploy` (see *Deploying the backend* below). **Platform firewall (ESLint-enforced, fail-closed):** every project carries one `platform:` tag — `platform:web` depends only on web + shared projects and never imports `firebase-admin`/`firebase-functions`; `platform:server` (`{{FUNCTIONS_PROJECT}}`, `firebase`) only on server + shared, never `firebase`/`@angular/*`; `platform:shared` only on shared, importing neither. A lint error naming `platform:` is a dependency crossing platforms — move the code, or classify the project with `{{NX}} g @bespunky/nx-tools:platform <project>` (inferred from its imports and explained; `--platform=` states it).

**Functions secrets (`defineSecret`) — nothing run locally can reach production.** Declare every secret in the committed `{{FUNCTIONS_ROOT}}/.secret.local.example` (one `KEY=` line each). Production's values live in `{{FUNCTIONS_ROOT}}/.secret.local` (**gitignored**) and have exactly one consumer: `{{NX}} run {{FUNCTIONS_PROJECT}}:push-secrets` sets each `KEY` in **Google Secret Manager** (values piped via stdin — never on a command line or in a log); add a line when your functions call `defineSecret('KEY')`, then re-push and redeploy. **The emulator never reads that file** — `tools/emulators.sh` writes the bundle's secrets itself on every launch and prints which mode it is in:

| Mode | What the emulator gets | How |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **inert** (default) | an `EMULATOR_INERT_<KEY>` placeholder for every declared key — the keys in `.secret.local.example`, in `.secret.local` (names only), and in every literal `defineSecret('KEY')` in the bundle | nothing to do; every tree, every agent worktree |
| **sandbox** (opt-in) | the values in `{{FUNCTIONS_ROOT}}/.secret.sandbox.local` (**gitignored**) — a test bot, a sandbox payment key, never production's; keys it lacks stay inert, and a value identical to production's is refused | create the file in **this** tree; `EMULATOR_SECRETS=inert` disarms it for one run |

Placeholders matter because the Functions emulator, given **no** value (or an empty one) for a declared secret, fetches the **real** one from Secret Manager whenever the suite runs under a real project id. As a second wall, the script points the emulator's Secret Manager at an unresolvable address, so anything a placeholder misses — a secret named dynamically, or a functions rebuild mid-session (`deleteOutputPath` wipes the bundle's copy: **restart the suite after `{{NX}} build {{FUNCTIONS_PROJECT}}`**) — fails loudly instead of acting as production. Secrets are **never borrowed across worktrees**: a worktree is inert unless someone puts a sandbox file in it. **Claude never creates or fills `.secret.sandbox.local`, and never sets `EMULATOR_SECRETS=sandbox`, without the user's explicit say-so** — arming an emulator with live credentials is the user's decision.

**Params (`.env`) — aim local runs at test targets with `.env.local`.** Non-secret params (`defineString`/`defineInt`, e.g. a chat id or webhook URL) live in `{{FUNCTIONS_ROOT}}/.env`, which deploys with them. Firebase's emulator-only override is `{{FUNCTIONS_ROOT}}/.env.local`: same keys, read **only** by the emulator, winning over `.env` (`firebase.json` → `functions.configDir` points both at the source directory, read in place). **Commit it**, so every tree's and teammate's emulator points at the same test targets: an uncredentialed call (a plain webhook) has no secret to make inert, so `.env.local` is what keeps it off production. The launch log lists the keys it overrides.

**`firebase init <feature>` needs an active project first.** This repo deliberately ships no `.firebaserc`, and `firebase init` with no active project templates the project id as the literal `undefined` — e.g. `firebase.json` → `auth.providers.googleSignIn.supportEmail: "support@undefined.firebaseapp.com"`. Run `firebase use --add` before any `firebase init`; if the string is already there, replace it with your project's real value (every upgrade warns while it remains).

### Deploying the backend — functions, rules & indexes

App Hosting ships the web app; it never deploys Cloud Functions, Firestore rules and indexes, or Storage rules. Those ship through **two Nx targets, both named `deploy`**, so one command deploys whatever a change touched:

| Target | Ships | Before it runs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `{{FUNCTIONS_PROJECT}}:deploy` | `firebase deploy --only functions` (the bundle in `dist/{{FUNCTIONS_ROOT}}`) | Nx builds `{{FUNCTIONS_PROJECT}}` (and lints it, where the workspace lints) through `dependsOn` — `firebase.json` has no `predeploy`, so nothing builds twice |
| `firebase:deploy` | the Firestore rules, Firestore indexes and Storage rules **`firebase.json` declares** (`tools/firebase-deploy-rules.mjs` reads it each run) — and nothing it does not declare | — |

```bash
{{NX}} affected -t deploy --project=<alias>                       # what changed since the base
{{NX}} run-many -t deploy --project=<alias>                       # everything
{{NX}} run firebase:deploy -P <alias>                             # one target: `nx run` takes --project itself, so use -P
```

**The road to a first deploy** — in order, each with how to tell it worked. `node tools/firebase-deploy.mjs --check` shows where this machine stands, and a deploy that fails prints the same road (both targets run the CLI through that owned runner), so nobody is left at Firebase's bare *"have you run firebase login?"*. `firebase` below is this project's own pinned `firebase-tools` — on PATH in the devcontainer through `node_modules/.bin`; outside it, run the same commands as `npx firebase …`:

1. **Log in** — a human, in a terminal (it opens a browser): `firebase login`. *Worked:* `firebase login:list` names the account. The login lives in `~/.config`, which survives a container rebuild.
2. **Pick the project** — `firebase use --add`: choose a project from the account and give it an **alias** (`prod`, `staging`, …); it writes `.firebaserc` — commit it — and makes that project the active one for this checkout (machine-local, so CI and teammates still pass `-P <alias>`). *Worked:* `.firebaserc` lists the alias and `firebase use` prints it.
3. **Deploy by hand** — `{{NX}} run {{FUNCTIONS_PROJECT}}:deploy -P <alias>` (Nx builds and lints `{{FUNCTIONS_PROJECT}}`, then `firebase deploy --only functions`) and `{{NX}} run firebase:deploy -P <alias>` (the rules and indexes `firebase.json` declares), or both at once with `{{NX}} run-many -t deploy --project=<alias>`. *Worked:* the CLI ends with *Deploy complete!* and a console link; `firebase functions:list -P <alias>` lists what is live.
4. **Hand deploys to CI** (optional) — {{#ci}}this project has the `ci` layer: *Continuous deployment (CI)* below takes it from here (the one-time cloud setup a human runs, then the two repository variables). *Worked:* `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` exists, and its first manual run goes green.{{/ci}}{{^ci}}`/bespunky-house:add-layer ci` renders a deploy workflow from the branch model's `ci` bindings (set one through the `bespunky-workflow:branch-and-release` skill first). Then a **human** runs `gcloud auth login` in a terminal and `! bash tools/setup-gcp.sh --environment <environment>` in Claude Code — it grants IAM, which Claude never does — and runs the GitHub commands it prints (the environment's protection, and its two variables). *Worked:* `gh variable list --env <environment>` shows both, and Actions → *Deploy* → *Run workflow* (scope `all`) goes green.{{/ci}}

- **Every extra argument reaches the Firebase CLI** (`--project=<alias>` / `-P <alias>` picks the `.firebaserc` alias). CI never passes arguments to every target: each Firebase `deploy` target carries a `ci-<environment>` configuration (`--project=<alias> --non-interactive`, written by the `ci` layer), and CI runs with `-c ci-<environment>` — a deploy target of anything else keeps its default. Arguments go **through a shell**: Nx joins them into one command line, each in double quotes, so `$…` and backticks in an argument are still expanded — escape them with a backslash (`-m 'cost: \$5'`), and never pass untrusted text as an argument. Deploy targets are **never cached**, **never run beside another task** (`parallelism: false`, so two deploys never race one Firebase project), and `{{FUNCTIONS_PROJECT}}:deploy` always **builds first** (`dependsOn: build`) — that is the deploy contract, and every upgrade re-asserts it (and says so if your target differed). Linting first is yours to drop.
- **`affected` sees exactly what ships.** `firebase.json`, `.firebaserc` and the rules files it declares outside `firebase/` belong to no project, so the deploy targets list them as `{workspaceRoot}/…` inputs — a rules-only commit deploys `firebase`, a `firebase.json` change both. The deploy runners (`tools/firebase-deploy*.mjs`) are **not** inputs: a toolkit release that only rewrites them deploys nothing. `firebase:deploy` **refuses** a declared rules file its inputs do not watch (one declared since the last upgrade): CI would never redeploy a change to it. The next upgrade watches it — or add the `{workspaceRoot}/<file>` input yourself. (Nx's `affected` is per project: a commit that changes a deploying project's own targets marks its deploy too — Firebase then skips the unchanged rules and functions.)
- **Deploy through Nx, never a raw `firebase deploy`** — that skips the build and ships whatever stale bundle sits in `dist/`.
- **Rules not in the repo yet?** Then `firebase:deploy` deploys nothing and says so: the console keeps them. To bring them under review and version control, pull the **live** ones in once — a human, in a terminal (it asks where to put each file; answer `firebase/<file>`): `firebase init firestore -P <alias>`, then `firebase init storage -P <alias>` — **two commands**: `firebase init` takes one feature, and silently drops a second. Each downloads the current rules (Firestore: and indexes) and declares them in `firebase.json`, so the first deploy changes nothing.
- **Rules the house seeded** (when the Firebase layer was added to a project without it) are **deny-all** and carry a `bespunky:house-seed` line; `firebase:deploy` **skips** a file that still has it, so the placeholder never reaches production. Write your rules (the emulators enforce them too), then delete the line. The house never seeds an **indexes** file: `firebase deploy --force` deletes every live index the file does not list, so the only indexes file that ever ships is one you wrote or pulled (`firebase init firestore`).

### Emulator seeds, caching & reset (no re-onboarding each serve)

The emulators **persist and seed their data**. The launch path is `tools/emulators.sh` (all `firebase:emulators*` targets funnel through it): it reaps stale processes, primes the **gitignored working dir** `.emulator-data/` from a seed, then starts the suite with `--import .emulator-data` and (full runs only) `--export-on-exit .emulator-data`. So session + data **cache across serves**: onboard once, stay in. Sign in with a seeded email and the Auth emulator matches the existing account by email — you inherit the seeded uid and its docs.

**A stop always saves.** The export runs inside firebase-tools and only when *it* is told to stop while its emulators are up — a signal that reaches an emulator JVM first is read as a crash and nothing is exported, which is exactly what Nx's stop (leaves of the process tree first, a force-kill ~5 s later) and a terminal's Ctrl+C do. So `tools/emulators.sh` runs the suite under a detached keeper, outside every tree, which stops firebase-tools exactly once however the stop arrives (the script being killed included) and checks that the export was written — a stop that saved nothing says so, loudly. The script prints `stopping — exporting emulator data to …`, progress, and `done … exported to …` (bounded by `EMULATORS_STOP_TIMEOUT`, 120 s). If whoever stopped it stops waiting first (Nx returns ~5 s after a Ctrl+C), the save still completes{{#web}}: `tools/dev/dev ps` shows the stack as FINISHING, and{{/web}}{{^web}}, and{{/web}} the next start of that suite waits for it. The suite's own log is `.bespunky/run/logs/<stack>.emulators.log`.

- **Seeds** are committed, known-good worlds under `tools/emulator-seeds/` (see its README for the catalog). They are **generated artifacts**, never hand-edited.
- **Reset (on-call):** `{{NX}} run firebase:reset` — takes effect on the next serve. Add `reset:<seed>` targets in `firebase/project.json` for extra worlds.
- **Rebuild seeds:** `{{NX}} run firebase:seed:build`.

**Directive — the seed is part of the schema contract.** The seeds' single source of truth is the **declarative** `tools/seed/world.mjs` (a description of accounts + Firestore docs — yours, never regenerated — applied by the generator-owned `tools/seed/apply.mjs`; adding a field/collection/world is purely additive, and the build orchestrator derives the seed list from it). **Whenever a Firestore document shape changes or a feature gains a backend, update the matching world in `world.mjs` and rebuild, committing `tools/emulator-seeds/`** — so the seed never drifts from the code.

**Seeding writes only where it is pointed.** `{{NX}} run firebase:seed:build` boots its own throwaway Auth + Firestore emulators (no Functions, so no trigger fires), applies each world and exports it — the only way seeds are built. The applier **refuses** to run without `FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST` and `FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST`: a bare `node tools/seed/build.mjs` would otherwise write into whichever suite owns the base ports — another worktree's, or a full suite whose triggers then fire real side effects. To put a seed into the stack you are serving, `{{NX}} run firebase:reset` and restart the serve; to write into a running stack on purpose, name its hosts (and `GCLOUD_PROJECT`) explicitly.

{{#web}}
### Auth in the devcontainer (the good news)

The shared co-driven browser is **real Chromium with a real window manager**, running inside the container and hitting **container-localhost directly** — so the old devcontainer OAuth pain is *gone*, and the previous "prefer redirect / popups fail" guidance no longer applies:

- **`signInWithPopup` works**, and so do redirect flows. There's no forwarded-port origin boundary between the browser and the app any more, so the `Sending authEvent failed` popup failure is gone — you don't have to prefer `signInWithRedirect`.
{{#angular}}- **`environment.ts` still ships an `authDomain`** — required for any OAuth provider sign-in (popup *and* redirect refuse without it, even against the Auth emulator). Don't remove it.{{/angular}}
- **VS Code Simple Browser / a host browser are now only *viewers*** of the shared browser over noVNC — they no longer have to *complete* the OAuth flow themselves; the containerized Chromium does. They stay fine for viewing.
- **Emulator auth works from anywhere** — any origin, any tree, including the pretty `<slug>.localhost` domains and offset ports.
- **Real Google OAuth is pinned to the main-tree serve on {{#angular}}`http://localhost:4200`{{/angular}}{{^angular}}the app's base origin{{/angular}}.** That exact origin/redirect URI is the one registered in the Google OAuth client; worktree offset ports and the `<slug>.localhost` domains are **not** registered, so real Google sign-in only works on the main tree at {{#angular}}`:4200`{{/angular}}{{^angular}}the app's base port{{/angular}}. To enable it, add {{#angular}}`http://localhost:4200`{{/angular}}{{^angular}}`http://localhost:<the app's base port>` (its `ports` in `.bespunky/dev.json`){{/angular}} to both **Authorized JavaScript origins** and **Authorized redirect URIs** on the OAuth 2.0 client in Google Cloud. For worktrees, use the Auth **emulator** (it authenticates anywhere).

**Only the app's port matters to the app.** The browser reaches every emulator through the app's own origin, so the forwarded emulator ports serve one thing: the **Emulator UI** (`:4000`) in a host tab, whose page dials each emulator (and the logging port) directly at its container port — so the UI is complete there only while those ports forward to the same number, and on a busy host it is the UI, never the app, that loses a panel. A worktree's **shifted** stack has its UI at `4000 + offset`, which the editor does not forward at all: open it in the shared browser. The serve banner says which of the two applies to the stack it starts. The shared browser reaches everything on loopback. The forwarded ports are read from `firebase.json` on every upgrade (never hand-edit them in `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`); if you re-port an emulator, change `firebase.json`{{#angular}} and the matching address in `environment.ts` (what server-side code dials){{/angular}}. When Claude needs *you* to complete a real OAuth sign-in in the shared browser, it enters **observe-only** (`shared-browser observe`) first so it won't move the view under you, and **resumes** after.
{{/web}}

**When the user asks to deploy this app — or otherwise connect it to a real Firebase project — walk them through these steps** (from the workspace root; the Firebase CLI is this project's own pinned `firebase-tools` devDependency — in the devcontainer `firebase` resolves to it through `node_modules/.bin` on PATH, elsewhere run it as `npx firebase …`). The BeSpunky default is Firebase **App Hosting** (framework-aware; config in `apphosting.yaml`), not classic static Hosting:

1. `firebase login` — one-time auth against their Google account (opens a browser).
2. `firebase use --add` — picks a project from their account and writes `.firebaserc`. If they don't have a project yet, create it first (Firebase console or `firebase projects:create <id>`).
3. `firebase apphosting:backends:create --project <projectId> --root-dir {{APPS_DIR}}/<APP>` — one-time: creates the App Hosting backend (interactive — picks the region, **links a GitHub repo** and its **live branch**). **`--root-dir` is required for an Nx app**: the prompt defaults to `/`, and from there App Hosting's Nx build fails to find a project. (To deploy without GitHub, `firebase init apphosting` instead — see below.)
{{#angular}}4. `firebase apps:sdkconfig WEB <appId> --project <projectId>` — prints the real web config for client-side SDK init.
5. Paste the returned `firebaseConfig` fields into `firebase` in **`{{APPS_DIR}}/<APP>/src/environments/environment.prod.ts`** — the production environment file, which the Angular build swaps in for `environment.ts` via `project.json`'s `targets.build.configurations.production.fileReplacements` (Angular's canonical environment-files pattern). Never edit `{{APPS_DIR}}/<APP>/src/app/firebase.config.ts` or its `firebase-<service>.config.ts` siblings — that's structural code the generator owns end-to-end and rewrites on every upgrade. *Where* you provide each service is your call and lives in `app.config.ts` / your lazy routes.{{/angular}}{{^angular}}4. `firebase apps:sdkconfig WEB <appId> --project <projectId>` — prints the real web config; wire it into the client the way that client's stack configures Firebase (this workspace has no Angular app, so no house generator owns that wiring).{{/angular}}

**Two deploy modes — know which one a backend is in.** *GitHub* (what step 3 sets up): every push to the backend's live branch rolls out — Firebase runs the build, this repo carries **no deploy workflow** to keep current (linking needs the repo on GitHub: `house.sh new --github`, or `gh repo create <name> --private --source . --remote=origin --push`). *Local source*: `firebase init apphosting` once (it adds an `apphosting` entry — `backendId`, `rootDir` = the app, `ignore` — to `firebase.json`, which every upgrade preserves), then `firebase deploy --only apphosting:<backendId>` uploads this workspace and builds it; no GitHub needed. **Which is live:** `firebase apphosting:backends:get <backendId> --project <projectId>` — the *Repository* column shows the linked repo, or is empty for a source-only backend. An Nx app builds from `{{LIBS_DIR}}/`, `tools/` and the root lockfile too, so never limit rollouts to changes under the app's own directory.

**Where `apphosting.yaml` is read from.** App Hosting walks **up** from the backend's Root Directory to the first directory holding any `apphosting*.yaml`, and reads `apphosting.<env>.yaml` **beside** that file. The house seeds them at the workspace root, which every backend under `{{APPS_DIR}}/` reaches. An `apphosting*.yaml` nearer to the app **shadows** the root files for that backend — the staging override included — silently; the upgrade warns when it sees one. Keep one home per backend.

**Everything else** — staging's Environment name, diagnosing a rollout that doesn't trigger or builds the wrong config, and **moving the project or its GitHub link to another Google/GitHub account or org** (the link lives in Developer Connect, where abandoned attempts pile up) — is the **`bespunky-house:firebase-app-hosting`** skill. Invoke it for any App Hosting work beyond the steps above.

**Never fabricate `.firebaserc` or the production config** — the Firebase CLI is the source of truth for cloud state.{{#angular}} `provideAppFirebase()` throws at bootstrap if production is attempted with an empty `environment.firebase`, so silent broken deploys are impossible.{{/angular}} Inside the devcontainer, **every interactive terminal shows a self-extinguishing banner** (`tools/firebase-welcome.sh`, sourced via `/etc/profile.d/zz-firebase-welcome.sh`) reminding the user of the recipe until setup is complete{{#angular}} — when both `.firebaserc` exists and `environment.firebase.projectId` in `environment.prod.ts` is filled, the banner goes silent automatically. The same recipe also lives in the header of `environment.prod.ts` and in the `bespunky-house:firebase-app-hosting` skill.{{/angular}}{{^angular}} — it goes silent once `.firebaserc` exists. The same recipe also lives in the `bespunky-house:firebase-app-hosting` skill.{{/angular}}

{{#angular}}
### Staging environment & per-environment Firestore database

A first-class **staging** environment is opt-in — `house.sh add-layer --staging firebase <project>` (or `nx g @bespunky/nx-tools:firebase-emulators --project=<app> --staging`). It scaffolds **`environment.staging.ts`** (swapped in by a `staging` build configuration in `project.json`) and **`apphosting.staging.yaml`**, which tells the App Hosting backend whose **Environment name** is `staging` to build `nx build <app> --configuration=staging` instead of the framework-default prod build. Bind it once, in the console — the backend → **Settings → Environment** → `staging`; `backends:create` has **no** flag for it. Until it is set (or if `apphosting.staging.yaml` is not beside the `apphosting.yaml` that backend reads), the staging backend silently ships **prod's** config.

**Isolating staging's data** — the `firebase.databaseId` field (in the Environment interface) picks which Firestore database a build targets. Set it (e.g. `databaseId: 'staging'` in `environment.staging.ts`) and `firebase-firestore.config.ts` uses `getFirestore(app, databaseId)` instead of the `(default)` DB. To turn it on: create the named DB in the console, set `databaseId`, and add it to `firebase.json`'s `firestore` array (`[{ "database": "(default)", … }, { "database": "staging", … }]`).

> **Caveat — same-project isolation is Firestore-only.** A named database isolates *client-written* data, but Auth users are shared (same project = one user pool), and the single shared Cloud Functions deployment writes via the Admin SDK's default `getFirestore()` → the `(default)` DB, so anything a callable persists still lands in prod. Full isolation (its own Auth pool + functions) needs a **separate Firebase project** — a deliberate step up in operational cost.
{{/angular}}

{{#angular}}
### Every emulator through the app's origin & Functions region

**The browser never dials an emulator port.** `firebase.config.ts` connects every emulated service — Auth, Firestore, Storage, Functions — to the page's **own origin**, and the generated **`{{APPS_DIR}}/<APP>/proxy.conf.mjs`** (the dev-server's `proxyConfig`, generator-owned) relays each one to the suite inside the container, **shifted by the stack's `PORT_OFFSET`**: Auth at its Google-API-shaped prefixes (`/identitytoolkit.googleapis.com/`, `/securetoken.googleapis.com/`, `/www.googleapis.com/`, `/emulator/`), Firestore at `/google.firestore.v1.Firestore/` (its streams) and `/v1/projects/<any id>/databases/` (its REST calls), Storage at `/v0/b/` (resumable uploads included — the relay rewrites their upload URL to a path), Functions at `/<any id>/<region>/…` (a Cloud Functions region: `us-central1`, `europe-west1`, …). Every route ends at a path-segment boundary and keys on no project id — `/emulators`, `/v0/blog` and `/<projectId>-landing` stay the app's, and whichever id the client runs under is relayed. The only app routes it would take are ones shaped like these (`/<x>/us-central1/<y>`). So:

- **A host tab needs only the app's port** — whatever host port the editor forwarded it to (`4200` → `4201` is fine), and a squatted emulator port on the host (common on Windows) can't stall anything.
- **A worktree needs no URL switch** — its dev server relays to *its* shifted suite; a tree can't reach another tree's emulators by accident.
- **Server-side code (SSR, if the app adds it) dials the container directly**: the address in `environment.ts`'s `emulators` block, shifted by the `PORT_OFFSET` the dev engine exports to a shifted stack. **Whether** a service is emulated it decides the way the page does: from the stack's URL switches, which the dev engine exports as `DEV_URL_QUERY` (`emulate=none` under `{{#nx-serve}}--no-emulators{{/nx-serve}}{{^nx-serve}}--skip=emulators{{/nx-serve}}`) — so the server render and the page it hydrates use the same backend. A per-tab override (`?real=auth`, localStorage) is that tab's alone: one server serves every tab, and an SDK instance connected to an emulator can't be disconnected per request. `emulatorEndpoint(service)` in `firebase.config.ts` is the one place that decides, for the browser and the server alike.
- **Your own proxy routes go in `{{APPS_DIR}}/<APP>/proxy.local.mjs`** — seeded once beside `proxy.conf.mjs`, never rewritten, merged after the house's routes (an `/api/` relay, say). A dev server that must keep a proxy config of its own includes the house's routes instead: `import { emulatorRoutes } from './proxy.conf.mjs'` and spread them into its export (`viteEmulatorRoutes` is the same table for a bare Vite `server.proxy`). An upgrade that finds a dev server with a proxy config of its own, or one it cannot configure, keeps it and says exactly what to add.
- **It is checked at startup.** In dev, the first emulated service asks the emulator hub through the page's origin which emulators run; a dev server that does not relay, a suite that is not running, or an emulator the suite lacks is a `[firebase.config.ts]` console error naming the fix — never a silent Firestore-offline hang.
- **http only.** The Firebase SDK reaches an emulator over plain http (Auth and Firestore could be pointed at https; Storage and Functions cannot), so a dev server served over **https** can't relay them: the app still loads, and the console says so at bootstrap — the same policy as the demo-config guard. Serve the dev server over http locally, or open the app with `?emulate=none` to use the real backend.

**Region** — pin callables to a region with `firebase.functionsRegion` (e.g. `'europe-west1'`) in an environment file; `firebase.config.ts` reads it for `getFunctions(app, region)`. Like `databaseId`, it's *configuration*, so it never means hand-editing the config file.

**`firebase.config.ts` is generator-owned and rewritten in full on every upgrade** — it holds no per-project values by design, so there's no "is it customized?" guess to freeze it behind template improvements. Change behavior where it belongs: **config** (emulator toggles, the `firebase` web config, `databaseId`, `functionsRegion`) in `environment.ts` / `environment.<env>.ts`; **providers** in `app.config.ts` beside `provideAppFirebase()`. Editing `firebase.config.ts` directly means the next sync silently reverts it.
{{/angular}}
{{/firebase}}

