## Continuous deployment (CI)

**`.github/workflows/deploy.yml` is generator-owned** (the `ci` layer) and rendered from the branch model's **`ci` bindings** — the table under *Branch & release parameters*. Never hand-edit it: **when** and **where** it deploys is the model, **what** ships is each project's `deploy` target.

- **On a push to a bound line** it deploys every project whose `deploy` target is affected since the last **successful** deploy of that line (`nrwl/nx-set-shas`) — a line *pattern* (`release/*`) deploys everything, since its branches share one environment — in that line's **GitHub environment**, with `-c ci-<environment>`: each deploy provider's targets carry that configuration (Firebase: `--project=<alias> --non-interactive`), and **only theirs** — give a deploy target of your own a `ci-<environment>` configuration to tell it about the environment. One deploy per **environment** at a time, **never cancelled** — a failed deploy is retried by the next push, and nothing in between is skipped.
- **Only protected lines deploy, one line per environment.** The branch model refuses a `ci` binding on a hotfix (work) line, a tag or a maintained release line, and a second line in one environment — and so does the generator.
- **Least privilege.** Install and `nx-set-shas` run in a job with **no** `id-token`; only the deploy job may mint a cloud token, and it never installs (it restores what the first job installed). Every action is pinned to a full commit SHA.
- **By hand:** Actions → *Deploy* → *Run workflow* on the branch; **scope `all`** runs every project's `deploy` (`nx run-many`). Use it for **the first deploy** and to recover an environment.
- **Node** comes from the project's Node file (`.nvmrc`, `.node-version` or `volta.node`), the install is the lockfile exactly — the same versions the devcontainer uses.
- **No workflow?** `.bespunky/ci.json` → `pending` says why: the model is undeclared, no line has a `ci` binding, or this repo already deploys from a workflow of its own (the house never writes a second one — delete yours to hand deployment over).
- **To change when or where it deploys** — add, move or remove a `ci` binding through the `bespunky-workflow:branch-and-release` skill (its change procedure), then upgrade; the workflow follows.
{{#firebase}}- **What ships here:** the Firebase `deploy` targets — Cloud Functions and the rules/indexes `firebase.json` declares. **App Hosting is never deployed by CI**: it is Firebase's own rollout (or a deliberate local-source deploy) — the `bespunky-house:firebase-app-hosting` skill. A one-off deploy by hand, only when the user asks for it: `{{NX}} run <project>:deploy -P <alias>`.

### The one-time cloud setup — a human runs it, never Claude

The workflow authenticates **keylessly** (GitHub's OIDC token, exchanged by Workload Identity Federation for a deployer service account — the house's own per environment, in a pool that holds nothing else). Creating that identity **grants IAM roles**, and Claude Code refuses IAM grants to agents **by design** — so `tools/setup-gcp.sh` (generator-owned, idempotent, every role justified inline, derived from what `firebase.json` deploys) is **run by the user**, in Claude Code with the `!` prefix — after logging gcloud in, in a terminal, as an account that may grant IAM in the project (`gcloud auth login`; the script refuses without it):

```bash
! bash tools/setup-gcp.sh --dry-run --environment <environment>   # every gcloud call it would make — changes nothing
! bash tools/setup-gcp.sh --environment <environment>             # create / converge (once per environment)
! bash tools/setup-gcp.sh --rollback --environment <environment>
```

The provider trusts a token **only** from this repository (by its immutable id), **only** in that GitHub environment, **only** on a `push` or a manual run of **this** workflow file, and **only** for the branches the model binds to it. **It records what it created and granted in `.bespunky/gcp/<environment>.tsv` — commit it**: re-runs converge to it, and `--rollback` removes exactly what it lists (never a grant that was there before; the deployed functions' run-time grants are left, with the command to remove each). At the end it prints the `gh` commands to run or paste back to Claude: the environment's **protection** (deployable only from its bound branches, after a required reviewer) and its two variables (not secrets). *Worked:* `gh variable list --env <environment>` shows both — then Actions → *Deploy* → *Run workflow* (scope `all`) is the first CI deploy, and it goes green (an unset variable fails it at a step that names the variable). **Run what an upgrade prints as `HUMAN_STEP:`** — a re-run when the bindings it was rendered for changed, and a `--rollback` when a binding was removed (the environment stays in the script, marked retired, until it is rolled back): the cloud side cannot be regenerated.

**For Claude:** never run `tools/setup-gcp.sh`, `gcloud iam …` or `gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding` yourself, and never try a workaround — hand the user the exact `!` line (from the `HUMAN_STEP:` output, or above) and say what to paste back.
{{/firebase}}

{{/ci}}
